ion, and requires corrections to survive +restart, rescan, and foreground/background reconstruction without silently +remapping ambiguous identities. The current correction schema and M2.6 +history-correction pause are identified as implementation prerequisites. +This is planning only: no correction behavior, migration, UI, test result, or +device validation is claimed. Next work is the durable correction contract, +reconciliation mapper, owner review UI, and their acceptance checks within M4.1. +The plan now defines exact successor mapping from reviewed evidence/visit support +sets, strict split/merge ordering for weak dates, an uncertain-memory assignment +entry, one effective owner-visible journey count alongside evidence-based +place/country counts, and a common validator for deterministic, AI-suggested, +and owner-initiated longer-gap merges. Ambiguous lineage and unknown order remain +pending for owner review. +Owner-initiated split/merge now has a distinct publication rule: validated +successors and counts appear in one atomic save, while predecessor-wide title, +date bounds and confirmation await scope review. Only a later ambiguous +automatic remap holds the last complete view. A rejected single-visit journey +cannot automatically re-enter a journey when new evidence arrives; its source +visit remains available and the owner can explicitly resolve or reverse it. +The M4.1 spec now fixes partial-overlap and duplicate-family remap outcomes, +separates `pending_scope_review` from `pending_remap` as durable correction +states, scopes negative constraints to reviewed support units and visit links, +and defines the save token, shared write guard, rollback and rebuild race rules. +An uncertain-memory state matrix keeps availability, trip membership and visit +support independent. These are planning contracts; the current schema and +adapter do not yet implement their lifecycle-generation or duplicate-family +requirements. +The follow-up planning pass adds ordered 