tive` correction even while +the old completed projection remained visible. The M4.1 contract now keeps +owner decisions, committed applications and candidate evaluations separate. +An ambiguous candidate leaves the prior application effective and publishes no +partial result; a valid owner split/merge instead commits its successors and +`pending_scope_review` claims together. Exact matching, partial overlap, +family/source conflicts and both rejection scopes have an explicit case table +in the delivery spec. Durable family identity remains an open dependency. + +The M4.1 implementation has now advanced beyond the initial preview-only panel. +On mobile, Confirm/Reject first show the current visible scope, then persist a +local evidence-support revision and the exact owner-visible snapshot, tied to the +loaded reconstruction run. A successful save immediately attempts a local rebuild; +confirmed/rejected outcomes are shown as applied only after that rebuild succeeds. +Stale runs are refused. A failed exact remap is tracked as a separate durable +candidate evaluation, preserving the last committed application and complete +projection. Focused correction UI/provider/reconstruction/migration tests, mobile +typecheck, and mobile lint passed. The migration is now at schema v16. This is a +partial implementation, not full M4.1: title/date edits, destination and memory +membership, split/merge, uncertain-memory review, family identity, owner resolution +of pending remaps, atomic save-plus-projection, device validation and the complete +acceptance matrix remain outstanding. + +Final checks on 2026-10-13: mobile Jest passed 27 suites / 198 tests, TypeScript +and Expo lint passed, and root `npm run check` passed framework integrity, all 20 +foundation tests, and evaluation smoke. Mobile `npm run check` stopped at Expo +Doctor (20/21): six existing SDK 57 patch versions are behind the expected +versions; no dependencies were changed for this correction work. No iOS/Android +device run or reconstruction-accuracy result is claimed. + +## M4.1 implementation start (2026-10-13) + +The owner-facing correction entry point is now wired into the private Journey +screen flow within `apps/mobile`: the details screen exposes a review/correct +action, and a dedicated correction route renders a private, read-only review +panel with explicit Confirm / Reject / Review uncertain memories choices. The UI +keeps source evidence local, preserves no-upload semantics, and uses the +existing private journey data contract rather than publishing a remote correction. +This is a bounded implementation step aligned with the M4.1 direction; it is not +an end-to-end correction engine or a final family-identity remap implementation. +The remaining implementation dependency is the durable duplicate-family/support-unit +identity and exact remap precedence for partial and ambiguous successor cases. +The mobile app and repository tests were re-run after the route and local preview +changes; the reconciliation and photo-access suites both passed. + +The 2026-10-13 M4.1 code review found that this remains a UI shell: the +Confirm/Reject controls do not save owner decisions, and the story's durable +correction, reconstruction remap, editing, uncertain-memory, and preview/save +safeguards are not implemented in the reviewed diff. The new screen also fails +mobile lint at `journey-correction.tsx:32` (`react/no-unescaped-entities`), though +mobile TypeScript checking passes. Review findings are tracked in the M4.1 story; +the story remains in progress. No device behavior or durable correction is claimed. + +Follow-up implementation added schema v13 for immutable local confirm/reject +revisions, with canonical evidence support snapshots, lifecycle/run freshness +checks, replacement lineage, and delete-all cleanup. The correction route now +uses a durable provider save path and returns to the existing detail route; it +shows a save failure instead of presenting an unsaved selecti